{"_data": [{"contents": [{"type": "lesson_name", "content": ""}, {"type": "unit_name", "content": "Various Approaches to Modeling in Software Development "}, {"type": "position", "content": 2}, {"type": "origin", "content": "stub"}, {"type": "lesson", "content": 2}, {"type": "level", "content": "A2.2"}], "type": "metadata"}, {"contents": [{"type": "test", "content": "false"}], "type": "achievment_test"}, {"contents": [{"type": "text", "content": "These are words you use when you talk about modeling in software development."}], "type": "target_word_describer"}, {"contents": [{"type": "primary", "content": "Describe common activities at work to a visitor using short sentences in familiar spoken and written situations.  Give detailed information related to UML and common related topics."}, {"type": "secondary_1", "content": "Identifies the expressions and vocabulary necessary for the description of places of work and jobs in common written and spoken situations.  Describes common activities related to Software Programming through the use of appropriate expressions, grammar and vocabulary in written or spoken situations according to the abilities related to this level."}], "type": "objectives"}, {"type": "background_image", "content": "enpe7n4le02ob02re1img01.jpg"}, {"contents": [{"type": "name", "content": "complex"}, {"type": "definition", "content": "multifaceted, compound or robust"}, {"type": "part_speech", "content": "(adjective)"}, {"type": "example", "content": "Example: I wasn't considering complex modeling tools or software based-systems, but I read about them, and personally, I think they're more practical."}, {"type": "audio", "content": "enpe7n4le02ob02aud01"}], "type": "target_word"}, {"contents": [{"type": "name", "content": "scheme "}, {"type": "definition", "content": "an outline, draft or model"}, {"type": "part_speech", "content": "(noun)"}, {"type": "example", "content": "Example: I don't like to draw schemes or use blackboards. I prefer to use more up-to-date approaches."}, {"type": "audio", "content": "enpe7n4le02ob02aud02"}], "type": "target_word"}, {"contents": [{"type": "name", "content": "research "}, {"type": "definition", "content": "a group of information that is put together for reference"}, {"type": "part_speech", "content": "(noun)"}, {"type": "example", "content": "Example: I was doing a little research on it last week."}, {"type": "audio", "content": "enpe7n4le02ob02aud03"}], "type": "target_word"}, {"contents": [{"type": "name", "content": "perform "}, {"type": "definition", "content": "to do, execute or develop   "}, {"type": "part_speech", "content": " (verb): "}, {"type": "example", "content": "Example: Documentation CASE allows us to perform software development, but no working code, it's like a virtual sketch."}, {"type": "audio", "content": "enpe7n4le02ob02aud04"}], "type": "target_word"}, {"contents": [{"type": "name", "content": "allow"}, {"type": "definition", "content": "to permit or authorize someone to do something "}, {"type": "part_speech", "content": "(verb)"}, {"type": "example", "content": "Example: They allow us to work with source code and even start engineering software"}, {"type": "audio", "content": "enpe7n4le02ob02aud05"}], "type": "target_word"}, {"contents": [{"type": "name", "content": "tool "}, {"type": "definition", "content": "an element that you can use to create or modify something "}, {"type": "part_speech", "content": "(noun)"}, {"type": "example", "content": "Example: We could talk to our boss and tell him that we want to adopt integrated CASE, the more tools we have the better."}, {"type": "audio", "content": "enpe7n4le02ob02aud06"}], "type": "target_word"}, {"contents": [{"type": "text", "content": "Look for more information about modeling approaches in software development.  "}, {"type": "text", "content": "Write characteristic of each approach.  "}, {"type": "text", "content": "Create a comparative chart where you include all the approaches.  "}, {"type": "text", "content": "Share your chart with the class. "}], "type": "takeaway_extension"}, {"contents": [{"type": "text", "content": "I understand the characteristics of different modeling approaches."}, {"type": "text", "content": "I compare different modeling approaches. "}, {"type": "text", "content": "I write information about modeling approaches. "}, {"type": "text", "content": "I share my work with the class. "}], "type": "takeaway_assessment"}, {"contents": [{"type": "heading", "content": "Past Continuous in Negative"}, {"type": "describer", "content": "We use the<b> past continuous </b>to talk about an action that didn't take place in the past during a specific period of time. When an action in the past was interrupted, or when two actions took place at the same time."}, {"type": "text", "content": "In order to use the <b>past continuous in negative</b>, you need to use the following structure: </br></br>  <div class=\"center_text\"> Subject + <strong>was/were</strong>  + <strong> not</strong>  + <strong> main verb (-ing form) </strong>  + Complement</div></br></br>  Examples: </br></br> <i> The customer <strong>was not asking</strong> for an update, he was trying to see what the problem was. </br> The engineer <strong>was not writing</strong> code when his boss arrived.</i> </br></br>  <strong>Note</strong>: remember you can also use the contractions of the auxiliary verbs: <b>wasn't</b> and <b>weren't</b>.</br></br>  Examples: </br></br><i>  I <strong>wasn't taking</strong> notes because I know every code by heart. </br> She <strong>wasn't paying </strong> while the coordinator <b>was explaining</b> the new approach.</i>"}, {"type": "example", "content": "I wasn't expecting the project to start so soon, so I was worried and decided to find some information about it. "}, {"type": "example", "content": "I saw some of our colleagues the other day, and they were not using paper-based modeling, they were using test driven design."}], "type": "learning_points"}, {"contents": [{"type": "heading", "content": "Verbs + Infinitives Review"}, {"type": "describer", "content": "There are some verbs in English that require you to use the <strong>infinitive</strong> of the next verb in the same sentence. "}, {"type": "text", "content": "You can use the <strong>infinitive</strong> to show that you have the intention or purpose to do something else. To use the infinitive, you need the preposition <b>to</b> and the main verb in base form (that is, without any changes). </br></br>  Example: </br></br> <i> John wants<strong> to write </strong>code on his computer.</i> </br></br>  Also, some verbs require you to use the infinitive of the verbs. </br></br>  Here's a  list of some of the most common verbs that need the infinitive after them: </br></br>  \u2022\tAfford</br> \u2022\tAllow</br> \u2022\tChoose </br> \u2022\tClaim</br> \u2022\tHave</br> \u2022\tHope </br> \u2022\tLearn</br> \u2022\tManage</br> \u2022\tIntend</br> \u2022\tPrefer</br> \u2022\tTry </br> \u2022\tWant</br> \u2022\tHelp</br> \u2022\tDecide</br> \u2022\tNeed</br></br>    Examples: </br></br> <i> Amy <strong>intends to finish</strong> coding today. </br> Shelly<strong> chose to use</strong> UML among other tools. </i></br></br>  In negative sentences, you use <b>not</b> before <b>to</b>. </br></br> Examples: </br></br> <i> Complex modeling tools <strong>help</strong> us <strong>not to waste</strong> time in planning. </br> Our boss wants us <strong>not to improvise</strong></i>."}, {"type": "example", "content": "They allow us to work with source code and even start engineering software."}, {"type": "example", "content": " We could talk to our boss and tell him that we want to adopt integrated CASE, the more tools we have the better."}], "type": "learning_points"}], "type": "sena", "labs": [{"stimuli": [{"template_override": 1, "type": "welcome", "_data": [], "contents": [{}], "id": 0}, {"template_override": 1, "contents": [{"value": {"images": [{"time": "100", "image": "enpe7n4le02ob02re2img01.jpg", "id": 0, "text": "<strong>Sean</strong>: Hi, Felicia! Were you programming yesterday? </br>\n<strong>Felicia</strong>: No, I wasn't programming anything. I was thinking about the project and which modeling tool we are going to use for it. </br>\n<strong>Sean</strong>: I have some ideas. I wasn't considering complex modeling tools or software based systems, but I read about them, and personally, I think they're more practical. </br>\n<strong>Felicia</strong>: I don't like to draw schemes or use blackboards. I prefer to use more up-to-date approaches. </br>\n<strong>Sean</strong>: Yeah, I saw some of our colleagues the other day, and they were not using paper-based modeling. They were using test driven design or TDD, in which we need to write some code to perform some tests. However, I think it wouldn't work for this project. </br>"}, {"time": "46500", "image": "enpe7n4le02ob02re2img02.jpg", "id": 1, "text": "<strong>Felicia</strong>: I agree. What about drawing tools or different types of CASE? </br>\n<strong>Sean</strong>: Drawing tools are the same as sketching or paper-based modeling options, but using computer programs to draw; so I don't want to use them. On the other hand, CASE could be a better option. CASE stands for Computer Aided System Engineering. I was doing a little research on it last week. I wasn't expecting the project to start so soon, so I was worried and decided to find some information about it. </br>"}, {"time": "80000", "image": "enpe7n4le02ob02re2img03.jpg", "id": 2, "text": "<strong>Felicia</strong>: Oh, I remember now. There are three main types, right? They are documentation, generative, and integrated CASE, right? </br>\n<strong>Sean</strong>: Yes. Documentation CASE allows us to perform software development, but no working code, it's like a virtual sketch. On the other hand, I was thinking about the possibility of using either generative or integrated CASE. They allow us to work with source code and even start engineering software. Integrated CASE also permits documentation, making it more complete. What do you think? </br>\n<strong>Felicia</strong>: We could talk to our boss and tell him that we want to adopt integrated CASE, the more tools we have the better."}], "coverimg": "enpe7n4le02ob02re2img01.jpg", "audiosrc": "enpe7n4le02ob02re2aud01"}, "type": "slideshow"}], "type": "audio", "_data": [], "id": 1, "title": ""}], "exercises": [{"stimuli_ids": [0], "template_override": 1, "questions": [], "type": "WLC", "_data": [{"contents": [{"type": "Learning Outcomes", "content": "I can talk about various approaches to modeling in software development. "}, {"type": "Learning Outcomes", "content": "I can compare different approaches to modeling software."}, {"type": "Learning Outcomes", "content": "I can give detailed information related to different approaches to modeling software."}, {"type": "Free Header Item", "content": "Do you prefer working on paper or working on a computer when modeling software? Explain."}, {"type": "Free Header Item", "content": "What software modeling tools do you know?"}], "type": "instructions"}, {"contents": [{"type": "group_name", "content": "Welcome"}], "type": "group"}, {"type": "feedback", "content": {"attempt_feedback": [{"type": "text", "content": "You have 2 tries"}, {"type": "text", "content": "you have 1 attempt left"}], "correct_feedback": "Correct", "incorrect_feedback": [{"type": "text", "content": "try again"}, {"type": "text", "content": "Maybe next time"}], "allowed_attempts": 2}}, {"type": "free_header_text", "content": "Ask your partner:"}, {"type": "free_type_text_area", "content": "Paper-based or digital?"}], "img_path": "", "id": 0}, {"stimuli_ids": [1], "template_override": 1, "questions": [{"_data": [{"contents": [{"type": "text", "content": ""}], "type": "question"}], "responses": [{"correct": 0, "selected": 0, "_data": [{"type": "text", "content": ""}], "id": 0}, {"correct": 1, "selected": 0, "_data": [{"type": "text", "cont